# KNOWN GAP (todo/t0729): on `--backend=llvm`, a nested `match` over a nested
# `Option` emits an aggregate copy as a raw `memcpy` whose SOURCE AND
# DESTINATION OVERLAP. That is undefined behaviour — `memcpy` requires
# non-overlapping ranges — and ASan reports it:
#
#   ERROR: AddressSanitizer: memcpy-param-overlap: memory ranges
#     [0xfffff103f7a8,0xfffff103f7c0) and [0xfffff103f7b8,0xfffff103f7d0) overlap
#     #0 __interceptor_memcpy
#     #1 main
#   Address ... is located in stack of thread T0
#
# Two stack slots 0x18 bytes apart, copied 0x18 bytes — they overlap by 8. The
# LLVM backend emits `call ptr @memcpy(ptr %dst, ptr %src, i64 N)` for aggregate
# copies (`declare ptr @memcpy(ptr, ptr, i64)`) rather than a move-safe copy, so
# a copy between two slots that alias is silently ill-formed. Today it happens
# to produce the right answer; nothing guarantees that, and it is exactly the
# class an optimizer is entitled to break.
#
# ── THE C LANE IS THE ORACLE AND IS CORRECT ──
# `gg build --sanitize` (default backend) builds this, runs ASan-clean under
# `detect_leaks=1`, and prints `inner-none`. That is the intended behaviour on
# BOTH backends. The defect is LLVM-lane-only.
#
# ── WHY THIS WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL NOW ──
# It is a PRE-EXISTING defect, not a new one: the emitted `.ll` is byte-identical
# with and without `--sanitize` (verified by diffing the two), so nothing about
# the sanitizer causes it. It went unseen because the LLVM lane had no working
# sanitizer at all — `--sanitize` was silently dropped under `--backend=llvm`,
# and `tests/security.rs` never read the backend selector, so the CI job named
# "Security tests (LLVM + ASan + UBSan)" was a verbatim re-run of the C suite.
# The first genuine run of that lane found this within ~8 minutes. It is the
# argument for the gate, in one fixture.
#
# ── SHAPE NOTES ──
# Two nesting levels are enough; `Option[Option[Option[int]]]` reproduces
# identically (that is how it first appeared, in
# `tests/fixtures/security/attack_64_deep_option_unwrap.gg` and
# `attack_70_inline_none_nested.gg`, which are the two suite fixtures this
# defect reddens). A plain `.unwrap()` chain over the same type does NOT
# reproduce — the nested MATCH is load-bearing, so do not simplify it to
# unwraps.
#
# INTENDED on both backends, sanitized: stdout `inner-none`, exit 0, no ASan
# report. GRADUATES to a live regression fixture the round t0729 is fixed.

void main():
    Option[Option[int]] p = Some(None())
    match p:
        case Some(a):
            match a:
                case Some(b): print(b)
                case None(): print("inner-none")
        case None(): print("outer-none")
